In article <10050009@hpcuhb.cup.hp.com> marty@hpcuhb.cup.hp.com (Marty Freitas) writes:
>It does not.  "Sated" to me means not hungry at all, not full to the
>point of vomiting and choking on it.  And how often does anyone do
>that anyway?  The Romans has Vomitoriums, I doubt they'd do that if
>they collected dead bodies.
>
First, the Vomitoriums weren't places to go when one was so full one threw up;
the Romans went back there, and stimulated the vomit reflex, typically by 
sticking feathers down their throats.  

Secondly, Satiate:
1) 	to fill; to satisfy the appetite or desire of completely; to feed to the
	full; 
2)	to glut; to gratify with more than enough, so as to weary or disgust
3)	to saturate

Sounds rather full to me...  in any case, the moral here is obvious:  if it
says satiated, don't eat.  If there's some corpse or other that you really
must devour, go up a level and burn off some calories...  The only time I've
died of choking in recent times (the last 2 months or so) was when I resumed
a saved game, and ate something shortly thereafter (I think a newt, but I'm
not sure as to what anymore).  Unfortunately, I think I ate a dragon or some
such right before saving, and it was enough to throw me past the choking part.
Alas, it never even asked if I wanted to keep eating (never did during the 
entire game, including when I was partaking of the dragon).  Ah well, easy
come, easy go...

